tgs:Interview.TGS_89_SianSutherland a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sian Sutherland: \"Innovating the Business of Plastics\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Sian Sutherland, co-founder of A Plastic Planet, exploring her own real, deliberately pro-business, pro-solutions approach to the plastics crisis — organized around a real, direct three-part framework: reduce, refill, and replace, aimed squarely at businesses, media, and policymakers rather than individual consumer guilt alone."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-09-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SianSutherland ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/89-sian-sutherland> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jTs6KejhSg4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/TGS89SianSutherlandTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan, tgs:Interview.TGS_37_MartinScheringer, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Sutherland directly grounds the plastics crisis in a real, striking personal-exposure statistic: the average person consumes roughly a credit card's worth of plastic every week, with real, documented plastic already found in human blood and placental tissue. She's real and direct about a genuine structural failure in recycling as a solution: plastic can typically only be recycled once or twice before quality degrades too far for further use, with roughly only 5% of US plastic actually getting recycled in practice, framing 'recyclable' labeling as functioning more as reassurance than accurate description. A real, pointed environmental-justice dimension closes her systemic critique: wealthy nations export a real, substantial share of their plastic waste to lower-income countries, with real, documented cases like a single Ghanaian town receiving roughly 15 million discarded clothing items weekly — framing plastic pollution as a real, direct extension of broader resource and waste imperialism, not a geographically neutral problem. Closes on a real, direct, live moment of connection-building: Nate proposes a future Reality Roundtable specifically pairing Sutherland with Shanna Swan, Pete Myers, and Martin Scheringer (already built), with Sutherland's own real, enthusiastic agreement — a real, direct, on-record origin point for what became RR-23, already built in this graph."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise almost entirely encyclopedic/institutional (endocrine disruptors, waste imperialism, fast fashion) rather than pointing to further individuals — Shanna Swan and Martin Scheringer are both directly cross-referenced and already built, not new finds."@en ;
    .


